  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 PM EST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nGOES-East Day Cloud Phase imagery shows only shallow bands of clouds \nstreaming off the Great Lakes now besides some high cirrus. KMQT \nradar returns continue to trend weaker and with less coverage, but \nrain showers (and a few snow showers above 1000 ft MSL) are hanging \non more than virtually all available model guidance suggested. The 2 \ninches of snow depth at WFO MQT will linger tonight as temperatures \nare unlikely to break 40 today (NBM probability >40 around 10%). RAP \nanalysis shows a relaxing pressure gradient as a vertically stacked \nupper low over the Lower Peninsula supports a weakening 1010mb low \nover New England while upstream ridging builds a ~1025mb high \npressure from northern Ontario down into the Central to Southern \nPlains. This is helping the winds relax some, especially in the \nwestern UP, though the eastern half of the UP still is experiencing \na few gusts of 15-30 mph.\n\nAs high pressure continues to move over the region to begin the \nweekend, precipitation ends this evening and skies clear out some \ntonight, leading to one of the coolest overnight low temperatures of \nthe season (NBM lows in the teens in some locations with 20s \nelsewhere). Temperatures this weekend warm to the 40s and low 50s, \nwhich should melt any remaining snow. Upstream, a shortwave trough \nwill quickly traverse central Canada, arriving in northwestern \nOntario around 06Z Sunday. The resulting clipper low and associated \nsynoptic precipitation look to miss all but the furthest eastern UP \nlocations, though sub-freezing 850mb temperatures over lake \ntemperatures around 10 C will reintroduce some lake effect/upslope \nrain into the forecast, with NBM PoPs of 15%-60% for the east half, \ndepending on proximity to the best low level cyclonic flow. The \nrapid pressure rises behind the surface low (~4mb/6hr) and cold \nadvection aloft will help mix down some gusty winds aloft to the \nsurface, with Euro ensemble probabilities of 40+ mph gusts Sunday \nafternoon of 20-45%. \n\nThe cycle repeats itself to begin next week. Ridging in the wake of \nthis clipper will result in another dry period with lighter winds \nlate Sunday night through Tuesday morning. The next shortwave in the \nclipper train looks to arrive Tuesday afternoon, though once again \nthe low looks displaced too far north and too weak to drive synoptic \nprecipitation for the UP but cooler air in the wake of the clipper \nwill bring lake effect precipitation chances (up to 30% per NBM) and \ngusty wind chances Tuesday (up to 50% chances of 30 mph gusts per \nEuro ensemble). Ensemble spread and forecast uncertainty increases \ndramatically late in the week, though the CPC outlooks 6-14 days out \nprefer a warm and dry pattern.",
